foreign  welcome to another installment of Harry  Potter Theory today we're going to be  discussing religion and Christmas in  Harry Potter peeling back the layers to  uncover if Christmas means the same  thing in the magical world as it does in  the Muggle World we'll also be more  broadly addressing the topic of religion  in Wizarding Society including its  prevalence diversification and whether  or not witches and wizards celebrate  god-like figures we're going to start  with Christmas then segue into religion  let's get into it Christmas time at  Hogwarts meant that it was time for  Hagrid and the other professors to  outfit the Great Hall with Christmas  decorations and 12 large decorated fur  trees mistletoe and Holly were among the  decorations that Hagrid and the other  professors helped put in the Great Hall  for the Christmas holidays on the  surface Christmas in Harry Potter  appears to be celebrated very much the  same way that it celebrated in mukul  society with with eggnog carols lights  trees presents and Christmas feasts  Harry had never in all his life had such  a Christmas dinner a hundred fat roast  turkeys mountains of roast and boiled  potatoes platters of chipolatas terrines  of buttered peas silver boats of thick  Rich gravy and cranberry sauce and  stacks of wizard crackers every few feet  along the table in fact the emphasis  placed on Christmas traditions in Harry  Potter is so significant that the  franchise in some ways has transcended  the page becoming immortalized in the  real world as a story that's become  synonymous with Christmas itself as soon  as the snowy season hits It's inevitably  time to hunker down in some blankets and  throw on Harry Potter but besides all of  the warm jumpers gingerbread houses and  Christmas pudding what is the actual  significance of Christmas in Harry  Potter is Christmas and Harry Potter  driven by religion or is it a secular  holiday first I'm going to take a look  at What Christmas Means to witches and  wizards then I'm going to dive into the  role of religion in The Wizarding World  as a whole from our perspective pretty  much everyone in Harry Potter seems to  celebrate Christmas if there was a  character from another faith that  celebrated some other form of winter  holiday you'd expect that Harry would  have heard about it but provided that  Christmas was traditionally a religious  holiday and that seemingly everyone  celebrates it in the story does that  mean everyone's a Christian this is  going to have a multi-part answer the  first thing I'd like to point out is  that Christianity is mentioned in Harry  Potter the story is certainly not devoid  of any religious references for example  the fat Friar Hogwarts ghost was a  wizard and part of the church who was  executed when senior Churchman grew  suspicious of his supernatural abilities  specifically his ability to cure pox by  poking peasants with a stick and his  habit of pulling rabbits out of the  communion cup JK Rowling has also  alluded to the fact that the books  explore Christian themes of sacrifice  and Resurrection specifically  referencing the Tombstones found in  godric's Hollow to me the religious  parallels have always been obvious  Rowling said but I never wanted to talk  too openly about it because I thought it  might show people who just wanted the  story where we were going at the end of  her latest and final installment in the  series there are specific references to  Christianity and themes of life after  death and Resurrection at one point  Harry visits the grave of his parents  and finds two biblical passages  inscribed on two separate tombstones  they are very British books so on a very  practical note Harry was going to find  biblical quotations on tombstones she  said but I think those two particular  quotations he finds on the Tombstones  they sum up they almost epitomize the  whole series the tombstone for Harry's  parents reads The Last Enemy that shall  be destroyed is death  and Ariana Dumbledore's reads where your  treasure is there will your heart be  also these are Bible quotes on the  Tombstones of witches and wizards yes  Lily Potter and Kendra Dumbledore were  muggle bonds but their funerals were  held by Wizards in The Wizarding World  the fact that there were religious  citations on their graves is likely  indicative of religious prevalence in  Wizarding Society however it could also  Simply Be Wizards choosing to honor the  known religions of the deceased I could  read into these quotes all day but  that's not the purpose of today's video  all I wanted to demonstrate is that  there are obvious references to  Christianity sprinkled throughout the  story which makes sense given that  Rowling is in fact a Christian with that  said it is highly likely that there are  witches and wizards in Harry Potter who  follow Christianity whether these  beliefs were adopted from the Muggle  world there's anyone's guess but given  the proximity of these two co-existing  worlds it's not surprising that one  would rub off on the other furthermore  plenty of Hogwarts students come from  the Muggle World which means they would  bring their belief systems with them to  Hogwarts supporting this is the fact  that Easter another religious holiday is  also celebrated in The Wizarding World  but does that mean Christianity is the  only religion in Harry Potter and does  it mean that all Hogwarts students are  Christians certainly not what we have to  remember about Christmas is that these  days it's less a religious holiday and  more just a cultural tradition  especially in Britain since the early  20th century Christmas has become a  secular holiday that's observed by  Christians and non-christians alike  devoid of any kind of Christian elements  those who follow Christianity May  celebrate Christmas the way it was  intended to be celebrated but for  everyone else it's more about what it  has become culturally a time to get  together with family exchange gifts put  up a tree and sit around the fire okay  so Christianity he is prevalent in  Wizarding Society but what about the  other religions the first direct quote  from rolling on the religion of witches  and wizards was in 2007 where she  revealed that Hogwarts is a multi-faith  school never specifying what those  faiths may or may not be following that  she responded to a tweet in 2014 where  she confirmed that there was a Jewish  Ravenclaw student by the name of Anthony  Goldstein and following that we got the  biggest piece of info on Wizarding  religion yet where she revealed there is  room for every religion belief system at  Hogwarts other than wiccans to everyone  asking whether their religion belief  non-belief system is represented at  Hogwarts the only people I never  imagined there are wiccans for those  unfamiliar with Wicca here's the  definition from Encyclopedia Britannica  Wicca the largest of the modern Pagan or  neo-pagan religions its followers who  are called wiccans typically identify as  a switches and draw inspiration largely  from the pre-christian religions of  Europe having publicly emerged in  England during the 1950s weka is now  found primarily in Western countries and  the number of wiccans is probably in the  low hundreds of thousands followers  often use the pentagram or five-pointed  Star as the main symbol of their  religion I believe that by condemning  wiccans Rowling is simply trying to  further distance herself from them  namely due to the Flack she gets for her  story and the magic in the story being  in a front on Christianity there are  many references to Witchcraft in the  Bible with almost all of them directly  condemning it and provided that one of  the key thematic elements of Harry  Potter is witchcraft it has been  unsurprisingly opposed by a certain  religious groups for example Deuteronomy  18 11 12 condemns anyone who casts bells  or who is a medium or spiritist or who  consults the dead anyone who does these  things is to testable to the Lord and  because of these detestable practices  the Lord your God will drive out those  Nations before you and exodus 22 18  states do not allow a sorceress to live  JK Rowling has insisted that magic and  Harry Potter is secular a product of  Science and not religion it does not  depend on active intervention by a God  or Gods this is consistent with her  condemnation of Wicca which believes  magic to be spiritual in nature even  though wiccans use terms like Wizard and  wedge it is not the same as what's  present in Harry Potter Rowling spoke on  this it's a different concept of magic  to the one laid out in the books so I  don't really see how they can co-exist  however regardless of rolling statements  and reassurances that the magic is  science-based the books are still a  no-go for Many religious families  hopefully as time goes on Rowling's  comments on this topic will alleviate  some of the fears experienced by  religious groups and open up the story  to more fans Okay so we've confirmed the  prevalence of Christianity and we've  also confirmed that the only religious  group excluded from Wizarding Society  are wiccans I mean it makes perfect  sense that there would be a plethora of  wizards with varying religious beliefs  present at Hogwarts especially if we  consider the fact that muckleborn  Wizards have been around since Hogwarts  was founded but what evidence do we have  of these other religions if we hone in  on Hogwarts in particular it doesn't  seem like being actively religious would  be all that easy there's no mention of  students attending a church or School  Chapel on Sundays and there's no mention  of bar mitzvahs or Muslim Students  prayer time perhaps these things are  prevalent in the magical world but  merely excluded due to the fact that we  are looking at the Wizarding World  primarily through the lens of Harry a  muggle-raised boy with a fairly narrow  perspective and that's it for today's  topic I hope you enjoyed it if you enjoy  the content please like the video and  subscribe to the channel until next time  remember to the well-organized Mind  death is but the next great adventure  foreign

